            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "obmcutil",
                    "line": 512,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Shawn McCarney",
                        "email": "shawnmm@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "smccarney"
                    },
                    "message": "Would it make sense to move all this new code into a new bash function? handle_cmd() is pretty long/complex as is."
                },
                {
                    "file": "obmcutil",
                    "line": 512,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "Yeah makes sense, let me move to it's own function"
                },
                {
                    "file": "obmcutil",
                    "line": 527,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Shawn McCarney",
                        "email": "shawnmm@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "smccarney"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm no bash expert, but should this comparison be like the following from line 621:\n```\nif [[ $host_timeout_target_state == \"masked\" ]]; then\n```\n\nThe single equal sign just looks strange as a long-time C/C++ guy."
                },
                {
                    "file": "obmcutil",
                    "line": 527,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "You're right - using == is clearer and more consistent with modern bash practices. While = works in [ ] for string comparison, == is more explicit and avoids confusion with assignment operators in other languages."
                }
            ],
